WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.859
This April, 19 million new cars were sold
in America — the highest level since 2005.

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:10.410
But these weren’t just any old cars.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:17.240
Today’s Chevy Silverados and Ram 1500s are
not quite the same as those sold a year or

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.270
even a few months ago.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.770
Something very strange is happening across
the industry.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:28.550
Features, packages, even certain parts have
suddenly disappeared from recently manufactured

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:29.550
vehicles.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.960
An exceptionally observant buyer of a ‘21
Silverado, for example, might notice their

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.690
truck getting about one less mile per gallon
than was advertised earlier this year on the

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.480
same model.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.110
Recent Peugeot 308 hatchback owners, meanwhile,
may be surprised to find the digital speedometer

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.489
shown on TV replaced with an old-fashioned
analog version.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:57.360
And the dashboard display on the Renault Arkana
mysteriously got a little bit smaller.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.750
It’s as if they’re playing a game: What
tiny things can they change without anyone

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.160
noticing?

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.380
The actual explanation is a global shortage
of semiconductors — the chips that power

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:15.049
virtually every electronic device, from rice
cookers to Xboxes.

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:19.729
Earlier this year, auto manufacturers found
themselves in an awkward position — the

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:26.229
average car is made up of about 30,000 individual
parts, yet they were missing one — worth

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:32.439
perhaps only 4 or $5, one-hundredth of a percent
of the final selling price.

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:33.639
It didn’t matter.

00:01:33.639 --> 00:01:38.009
Without one, single component, everything
had to wait.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:42.619
At first, factories simply stopped producing
and workers went home.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:48.759
Ford, Honda, Volkswagen, Audi, and GM all
shut down at least a few of their production

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:49.759
lines.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:55.899
Audi furloughed 10,000 workers, and Volkswagen
predicts it will sell 100,000 fewer cars as

00:01:55.899 --> 00:01:57.739
a direct result.

00:01:57.739 --> 00:02:03.959
The total damage to the industry could be
upwards of $60 billion, despite ordinarily

00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:08.390
spending only about 5% of its GDP on semiconductors.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:14.240
Eventually, though, many brands resigned to
cutting back features, realizing the problem

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.860
is here to stay.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.000
It’s not just cars.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.281
Sony doesn’t expect to meet demand for its
PS5 until next year, Nintendo can’t make

00:02:23.281 --> 00:02:29.160
enough Switches, and even deep-pocketed Apple,
usually immune to such disruptions, has been

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.160
affected.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.530
According to Goldman Sachs, semiconductors
touch 169 different industries, and the worst-case

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.240
scenario would be a 1% reduction in U.S. GDP.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.880
Given how strong the incentives are to alleviate
the shortage, what on earth could cause such

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.900
a costly disaster?

