00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.130
Early last year, demand for cars around the
world plummeted.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:09.500
At the same time, sales of home electronics
skyrocketed — routers, monitors, webcams,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.820
keyboards, iPads, and so on.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.870
Car companies canceled their semiconductor
orders, consumer electronics brands ordered

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.900
extra, and to the extent the two overlap,
production lines shifted to producing for

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.380
the latter.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:29.780
Automobile sales, however, jumped back much
faster than expected — almost as fast as

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.110
they had fallen in March.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:38.850
Already by September of last year, demand
for cars was near normal, 2019 levels.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:43.819
Not used to such wild swings in demand, when
Americans got back to their usual habit of

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:50.630
buying trucks the size of small European countries,
car manufacturers rushed to order more parts.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.320
The problem is that ‘semiconductor’ and
‘rushed’ are practically antonyms.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:01.150
There’s nothing fast about producing billions
of transistors on a single chip or maintaining

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:06.709
conditions 100,000 times more sterile than
a hospital operating room.

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:12.880
When you hear semiconductors have extraordinarily
long lead times, think not days or even weeks

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.170
but rather months.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.539
Many, many months.

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:23.949
A single chip takes no fewer than fourteen
and as many as 20 weeks just to manufacture.

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:29.569
After that, you can expect an additional 6
weeks for assembly, testing, and packaging.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:35.120
This, mind you, assumes the requisite machines
are sitting around, ready to start the moment

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.330
an order is received, and doesn’t include
delivery time.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.830
Ignoring these two giant caveats, each chip
will take three to six and a half months from

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.749
start to finish — longer than the entire
dip in car sales last year.

00:04:49.749 --> 00:04:54.860
But if it were that simple, supply would’ve
caught up with demand months ago.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.240
Instead, some analysts don't expect the current
shortage to end until later this year, or,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.919
even next year.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:04.760
Something else is happening here.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.879
The semiconductor supply chain isn’t just
slow.

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:11.340
It also involves many different parties.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:16.930
Steps which would be separated only by conveyor
belts or departments in other industries are

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:23.949
so complex for semiconductors as to justify
entirely different, highly specialized companies.

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:29.139
And, while efficient, specialization can also
cause problems.

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:35.210
Imagine, for instance, last September when
Toyota saw its cars start to fly off the lot

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.720
— it would’ve immediately adjusted its
sales forecast accordingly.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.870
If sales the previous month were 18 million,
it would’ve extrapolated a trendline and

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:48.449
added some cushion — ordering, let’s say,
20 million.

00:05:48.449 --> 00:05:53.430
But remember, Toyota’s order doesn’t just
go directly to a factory.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.289
Instead, it might go to a company like Panasonic,
which might be responsible for delivering

00:05:58.289 --> 00:06:00.529
the car’s navigation unit.

00:06:00.529 --> 00:06:05.809
Panasonic, on the other hand, will see an
order of 20 million units, extrapolate, and

00:06:05.809 --> 00:06:09.210
add some cushion — maybe ordering 21 million.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:16.430
It, likewise, may order from another intermediary,
who, again, will extrapolate and add slack.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:22.620
By the time an actual manufacturer, or “fab”,
receives an order, it may bear little resemblance

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.419
to the original.

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:30.210
Each step of the process involves an independent,
profit-maximizing firm, making the most logical

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.229
decision given its limited information.

00:06:33.229 --> 00:06:38.180
In aggregate, however, demand gets more and
more distorted.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:42.169
Under normal conditions, this effect is known
and manageable.

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:47.559
But when orders come flooding in all at once,
as they did in September, manufacturers are

00:06:47.559 --> 00:06:49.539
very easily overwhelmed.

00:06:49.539 --> 00:06:55.520
And because demand shot up everywhere at once,
every company was effectively competing for

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.050
the same, limited supply.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:03.669
Each one, trying both to minimize the risk
of under-ordering and maximize its priority

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:06.810
in line, inflated its order.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:12.099
For manufacturers, the difficulty is not so
much solving the shortage — with lead time

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:16.919
so long, there’s really nothing that can
be done except build more factories, which

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:18.770
takes years.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:22.499
Their real task is to project confidence.

00:07:22.499 --> 00:07:28.669
If they can credibly convince buyers of their
capacity, buyers stop inflating orders, and

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:31.999
at least some of the shortage will naturally
resolve.

00:07:31.999 --> 00:07:37.509
It’s fundamentally no different from toilet
paper, masks, or diapers.

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:43.479
If even one person fears a shortage, they’ll
start hoarding supplies, forcing everyone,

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:46.610
including the most rational, to play along.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:51.619
Shipping prices are at recent highs, and Chinese
companies may be hoarding supplies for fear

00:07:51.619 --> 00:07:57.999
of U.S. sanctions, but no one truly knows
how much is simply panic buying.

00:07:57.999 --> 00:08:02.749
In reality, the “shortage” may not be
much of a shortage at all.

00:08:02.749 --> 00:08:05.520
What if there was a shortage, though?

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.629
Supply may not be severely constrained now,
but if this is what merely believing it to

00:08:10.629 --> 00:08:14.430
be, looks like, what would it, actually?

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:19.240
The very nature of semiconductors all but
guaranteed the dominance of a small number

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.469
of companies.

00:08:20.469 --> 00:08:26.319
Opening a new factory easily costs upwards
of $10 billion, and as much as twice that

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:28.020
for the most cutting-edge.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:33.960
But because of how fast the underlying technology
changes, it will very likely only remain useful

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.380
for 5 or so years.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:40.680
That means burning through a million dollars
every five hours.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.660
There’s absolutely no room for mistakes
or downtime.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:51.160
Factories run 24/7, 365 days a year, with
no exceptions.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:56.050
With capital costs this high, it’s simply
not possible for new companies to dip their

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.070
toes in the market.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:04.990
Over time, the number of competitors has dwindled,
from 25 in 2002 to just three by 2016.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:11.950
The winner in each major chip technology revolution
reinvests its earnings back, ensuring they

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.050
dominate even harder in the next.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:20.170
With just a handful of competitors, the industry
is among the most consolidated in the world

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.200
— more so than airlines, biotechnology,
or soft drinks.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.720
While not, strictly speaking, a monopoly,
Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.950
is the clear winner in the contract foundry
market — which produce on demand for companies

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:37.440
like Apple and Toyota.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.330
That’s a lot of very important eggs in one
basket.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:50.030
Especially given that over 90% of TSMC fabs
are located in one, politically fraught country:

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:51.420
Taiwan.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:57.070
When semiconductors are flowing, no one pays
much attention to just how amazing their existence

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.710
is.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:04.400
But when they stop, when a single ship blocks
the Suez Canal, or when an oil pipeline is

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:10.050
held for ransom, the fragility of our modern
world quickly becomes apparent.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:15.450
And yet, it’s in part that very fragility
which makes it, in all but the weirdest of

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.390
times, so dependable.

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:27.470
TSMC needs Taiwan, Taiwan needs TSMC, and
at least for now, the world needs TSMC.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:32.020
